Sure, let's translate the provided text into UVM (Unified Verification Methodology) code. Note that UVM is primarily used for hardware verification and not typically for defining CI/CD pipelines. However, we can represent the described CI/CD logic using UVM-like constructs.

```systemverilog
class CI_CD_Pipeline extends uvm_object;
  `uvm_object_utils(CI_CD_Pipeline)

  // Define stages
  bit build_stage = 1;
  bit unit_test_stage = 1;
  bit staging_deploy = 0;
  bit production_deploy = 0;
  bit integration_test = 0;
  bit security_scan = 0;
  string deployment_strategy = "BLUE_GREEN";
  bit hotfix_bypass = 0;
  bit manual_qa_approval = 0;

  function new(string name = "CI_CD_Pipeline");
    super.new(name);
  endfunction

  function void configure_pipeline();
    if (staging_deploy) begin
      if (!unit_test_stage) begin
        `uvm_error("CI_CD", "Staging deployment failed due to Unit Test failure")
      end
    end

    if (production_deploy) begin
      if (!staging_deploy) begin
        `uvm_error("CI_CD", "Production deployment failed due to Staging failure")
      end
      if (!integration_test ||!security_scan) begin
        `uvm_error("CI_CD", "Production deployment failed due to Integration Test or Security Scan failure")
      end
      if (deployment_strategy == "CANARY") begin
        // Add Monitoring stage with Rollback trigger
        add_stage("Monitoring");
        add_trigger("Rollback");
      end
      else if (deployment_strategy == "BLUE_GREEN") begin
        // Double the infrastructure resources
        double_infrastructure_resources();
      end
    end

    if (hotfix_bypass) begin
      if (!security_scan) begin
        `uvm_error("CI_CD", "Hotfix deployment failed due to Security Scan failure")
      end
      if (manual_qa_approval) begin
        // Add Manual QA Approval step
        add_step("Manual QA Approval");
      end
    end
  end

  function void add_stage(string name);
    // Simulate adding a stage
    `uvm_info("CI_CD", $sformatf("Added %s stage", name), UVM_LOW)
  end

  function void add_trigger(string name);
    // Simulate adding a trigger
    `uvm_info("CI_CD", $sformatf("Added %s trigger", name), UVM_LOW)
  end

  function void add_step(string name);
    // Simulate adding a step
    `uvm_info("CI_CD", $sformatf("Added %s step", name), UVM_LOW)
  end

  function void double_infrastructure_resources();
    // Simulate doubling infrastructure resources
    `uvm_info("CI_CD", "Doubled infrastructure resources", UVM_LOW)
  end
endclass
```

This UVM-based class `CI_CD_Pipeline` encapsulates the logic described in the text. It includes methods to configure the pipeline based on the defined stages and strategies. Note that the actual implementation of adding stages, triggers, and steps would depend on the specific environment and tools being used.